    <name>
      <text>Integration by u substitution_Q2</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Find  \( \displaystyle \int {@f_x@}\, dx\)  by use of substitution method.<br></p><p>[[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]][[feedback:prt1]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><b>Worked Solution</b></p>
<p>Let us solve \( \displaystyle \int {@f_x@}\, dx\) by substitution method.</p>
<p> Let \(u=x^2-{@constant_denom@}\)</p>
<p> Differentiating \(u\) with respect to \(x\) we have: \(\frac{du}{dx}=2 \cdot x\).</p>.
<p>Making \(dx\) the subject, then \( dx=\frac{du}{2 \cdot x} \).</p>
<p>Replacing the obtained expressions \(u\) and \(dx\) in the integral \( \displaystyle \int{@f_x@}\,dx\), we get;</p>
<p>\[ \displaystyle \int {@f_x@}\, dx = \displaystyle \int {@g_x@}\, du \].</p>
<p>\[ \displaystyle \int {@f_x@}\, dx = {@tas@}\].<br></p>
<p>But \(u=x^2-{@constant_denom@}\)<br>
</p>
<p>Therefore,</p>
<p>\[ \displaystyle \int {@f_x@}\, dx={@ta_1@}\]</p>
<p>where \(c\) is the constant of integration. </p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
    <defaultgrade>1</defaultgrade>
    <penalty>0.1</penalty>
